## 1. Executive Summary

A production-oriented automated test foundation has been established for the `flutter_bloc`-based Rento client, following the Test Pyramid and dependency-isolation practices. In parallel, key business workflows have been exercised manually in real application scenarios across customer and office flows. Automated product coverage is still not representative, so the manual evidence and the automated coverage status should be considered separately.

**Key figures**

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Automated tests | 50 passing at bootstrap |
| Cubit coverage (sampled) | 4 of ~25 Cubits |
| Bloc coverage (sampled) | 1 of 5 Blocs |
| Line coverage (current) | To be measured and recorded |
| Static analysis | Existing infos/warnings to be counted; no new blocking errors introduced |
| Maturity | Level 2 of 4 |

**Assessment**

| Dimension | Rating | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Tooling and harness | Adequate | `bloc_test`, `mocktail`, golden testing, CI |
| State-layer coverage (Cubit/Bloc) | Partial | Reference implementations complete; product coverage not yet representative |
| Presentation isolation | Adequate (sample) | Widget tests via `MockCubit` + `whenListen` |
| Data-layer isolation | Adequate (sample) | Mocked repositories; no real network in unit tests |
| CI quality gate | Basic | format + analyze + test + coverage artifact |
| E2E on device | Deferred | Scaffold exists; blocked by local toolchain, not design |

**Conclusion.** The project has moved from ad-hoc tests to an extensible system. Manual scenario validation adds practical confidence in key booking, synchronization, network-recovery, settlement, and notification workflows. The remaining quality-engineering gap is to preserve these checks as auditable scenario records and automate the highest-risk cases, while expanding Cubit/Bloc coverage.

