## 5. Manual Business Scenario Validation

In addition to the automated test foundation described in this report, the Rento application has undergone hands-on validation using real application scenarios and varied test conditions across the customer and office workflows. The following business-critical areas were exercised manually:

| Scenario area | Manual validation focus |
|---|---|
| Concurrent booking attempts | Competing booking attempts for the same vehicle and the resulting availability/booking behavior |
| Cross-app booking state | Booking status changes and consistency between the customer application and office application |
| Network interruption and retries | Behavior during network failures, repeated actions, and retry scenarios |
| Pricing and settlements | Consistency of amounts and settlement outcomes between the involved parties |
| Notifications and state refresh | Notification delivery and booking-state updates after retry or recovery scenarios |

These hands-on scenarios provide practical evidence that important business workflows have been exercised beyond isolated unit-test samples. They complement, rather than replace, automated regression tests: manual validation can explore realistic combinations of conditions, while automation makes selected cases repeatable and enforceable in CI.

**Evidence boundary.** This report records that these scenario areas were manually exercised in real application flows. It does not claim a quantified pass rate, exhaustive coverage of every possible combination, or independent verification of backend concurrency guarantees. For auditability, the recommended next step is to retain a scenario log with preconditions, steps, expected and observed outcomes, environment/build, date, and any defects found or retested.

